{"kind":"request","text":"Make test grants explicit, and name the check behind each expected refusal\n\nHugh approved this one finding from report baa75642, 4 November 08:15 EST. Keep it small.\n\nProblem: Ledger.grants() in packages/derive/test/fixtures.ts gives every actor in the key set every action of the definition, unless a test presents other grants. Authority is therefore invisible in most judge tests. A test that presents narrower grants and expects a refusal cannot tell a missing grant from the business guard it means to check. The docs teach the same fixture to authors (docs/build-a-counter.md and docs/build-a-lending-library.md import from @generalbusiness/dap-derive/testing).\n\nGuarantee: a test that expects a refusal states which check refused it, the grant check or a named guard, and fails if a different check refused.\n\nMechanism: the smallest change that delivers that guarantee. For example, rename the all-actions default so a test that relies on it says so, and give the refusal assertion an expected reason. No new test framework, plan format or generator. Update only the callers the change requires, and the two docs if their examples change.\n\nWitness: one wrong-actor case where both actors hold the grant and the business guard refuses, and its pair where the grant is missing and the grant check refuses. Each assertion fails when given the other reason. Report how many existing refusal assertions did not name their check before the change.\n\nOrder: after 1c7dddfb, ahead of fd0cec33. The O4 live runs take precedence whenever the browser connection returns.","body":{"conditions":"Reviewed head lands on main; expected refusals name their check, with the paired grant and guard witness.","target_head":"c44e311c2a31396e5c194e2fb20a8a7824e58bd3","target_ref":"refs/heads/main","target_repo":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}